# Project Specifics
- Unless stated differently create all components as React components in src\business-apps folder
- Each component should extend its props from the `BusinessAppContextProps` interface.
- Whenever an access token is required, use it from the `BusinessAppContextProps` interface.
- Whenever an user, an user info or an employee ID is required, use it from the `BusinessAppContextProps` interface.
- Whenever an active user locale is required, use `getCurrentActiveUserLocale` from `src\common\utils\windowUtils.ts`.
- Whenever an active structure ID is required, use `getCurrentActiveStructureId` from `src\common\utils\windowUtils.ts`.
- Whenever text translation is required, avoid usage of plain text and use `getTranslationFromI18nKeyAndLabel` from `src\common\utils\windowUtils.ts` instead.

# Naming Conventions
- use kebab-case for folders (e.g. "src\business-apps\fin\expenses\personal-expenses"
- use PascalCase for files like apps, forms, and components (e.g. PersonalExpenses.tsx, EmployeeRecord.tsx)
- use camelCase for files like index.ts, hooks, utils, types, other functions, etc.

# Folder Structure for business apps
- folders in src\business-apps represent modules (e.g. hr, fin, crm etc.)
- folders in src\business-apps\<module> represent either sub-modules (e.g. core, absences, expenses, etc.) or shared resources for this module (e.g. `components`, `hooks`, `types`, `utils`, `queries`, etc.)
- folders in src\business-apps\<module>\<sub-module> represent either `apps` (e.g. personal-expenses, manager-expenses, employee-record, etc.), `forms` (e.g. expense-item-form, expense-request-form, etc.) or shared resources for this sub-module (e.g. `components`, `hooks`, `types`, `utils`, `queries`, etc.)
- files in src\business-apps\<module>\<sub-module>\apps\<app-name> are related to that single app where one `*.tsx` (PascalCase) with the name of app is located and an `index.ts` (camelCase) file with a named export of the component and a default export of the component.
- files in src\business-apps\<module>\<sub-module>\forms\<form-name> are related to that single app where one `*.tsx` (PascalCase) with the name of form is located and an `index.ts` (camelCase) file with a named export of the component and a default export of the component.

Folder Structure Examples for Apps and Forms:
- src\business-apps\hr\hr-core\apps\employee-directory\EmployeeDirectory.tsx
- src\business-apps\hr\hr-core\forms\employee-creation-form\EmployeeCreationForm.tsx

Folder Structure Visualization:
src
└──	business-apps
	└──	<module>/
		└──	<sub-module>/
			├──	apps/
			│	└──	<app>
			│		├──	AppName.tsx
			│		├──	index.ts
			│		├──	components
			│		├──	hooks
			│		├──	types
			│		├──	utils
			│		├──	queries
			│		└──	...
			├── forms/
			│	└──	<form>
			│		├──	FormName.tsx
			│		├──	index.ts
			│		├──	components
			│		├──	hooks
			│		├──	types
			│		├──	utils
			│		├──	queries
			│		└──	...
			├── components/
			├── hooks/
			├── types/
			├── utils/
			└──	...

- You must never exceed 300 lines of code per file (excluding imports and blank lines).
- When a file approaches this size, break out logic, UI, hooks, or utility functions into separate, logically named files.
- Prefer splitting code by component, feature, or responsibility to support clarity and reuse.
- If splitting is not obvious, prompt the user for suggestions on how to divide the code.

# Component Dependencies
- Use react for UI components
- Use tailwind v4 for all styling (utility classes)
- Use ant-design for React UI components
- Use @dnd-kit for drag and drop
- Use zustand for state management in React
- Use react-dom for working with the DOM
- Use typescript for type safety
- Use prettier for code styling
- Use eslint for linting

- Do not install other packages for UI themes, icons, etc. unless absolutely necessary or I request them.
- The list of required dependencies must be small and simple and must reuse existing packages.

- For all date and time related logic, use `dayjs` and import the plugins `weekday` and `localeData`.

# Component Development
- Always start all CSS classes strictly with prefix `gba:`, this means `gba:<tailwind-class>`.
- Always add to all HTML elements in a component the `data-testid` attribute with a value which is unique and descriptive.

- Use always the existing `DebouncedInput` component for search fields to provide debounced search.
- When tab components are used, provide a unique tab key for each tab and use this in a URL query parameter.

- Add all CSS files in `src/index.css`, so that they are included in `dist/assets/main.css` by the vite bundler. Only this way will they be applied on the NEWWORK platform.

- For Graphql API requests, use src\common\network\graphql\useGraphQLClient.ts. Analyze the file first, and its dependency.
- For REST API requests, use src\common\network\rest\useRestClient.ts. Analyze the file first, and its dependency.
- For all API requests to a service, check if already exists in src\common\network, or create a new folder for that entity type. Example: `src\common\network\catalog-entry`, `src\common\network\expense-plan` folders, named in camelCase, and exported as named exports.
- If the API request is REST, use a singleton client class that extends RestClient. Example: src\common\network\catalogs\CatalogManagerClient.ts
- If the API request is Catalog, search for existing hooks and use them, otherwise create a new one. Example: src\business-apps\hr\hooks\useGetContractTypes.ts
- In the singleton client REST class, the URL key is in the `.env` file. Example: `env('NEXT_PUBLIC_KOGITO_PROCESSES_ENDPOINT')`, NEXT_PUBLIC_KOGITO_PROCESSES_ENDPOINT is in `.env`.
- For all API requests, make sure accessToken exists.
- Never use hardcoded base URLs like "https://newwork-dev.newwork.com/<rest_of_the_base_url>". Always use `env` from '@/common/env/EnvUtils'.
- If a base URL does not exist, make sure to add it to the .env file, use `NEXT_PUBLIC_BASE_URL` or `NEXT_PUBLIC_API_SERVER` for the host part and concatenate the URL path in a new variable.

# Project Development
- Simplify the code as much as possible and keep it clean
- Make code changes minimal and focused
- Focus on clear separation of code and its responsibilities
- Make usage of existing code, functions or components
- Remove unused code
- Remove commented code
- Keep usage of `console.log` minimal and avoid `console.log` when user data, access tokens, responses or any other sensitive data are used
- Keep usage of code comments minimal and only describe React components, utility functions and complex code blocks
- Remove unused imports or imports that are not used
- Use the Conventional Commits specification for git commit messages

# Publishing
- apps and forms need to be published on a daily basis
- apps: Use npm run publish:screen -- /<module>/<sub-module>/apps/<app-folder> to publish an app
- forms: Use npm run publish:screen -- /<module>/<sub-module>/forms/<form-folder> to publish a form
- options: Use  `--resource` or `-r`: Resource URL mode - enables prompts for creating resource URLs (by default, direct publishing without prompts)

# Keep files small and maintainable
- No single file should exceed 300 lines of code.
- When a file approaches this size, break out logic, components, hooks, or utility functions into separate, logically named files.
- Prefer splitting code by component, feature, or responsibility to support clarity and reuse.
- If splitting is not obvious, prompt the user for suggestions on how to divide the code.
# Processes with native screens
- When wanting to continue a Kogito process by completing the current task, use `dispatchProcessContinueEvent(event, payload)` from src\common\utils\processEventUtils.ts
- `dispatchProcessContinueEvent` assumes that there is a native screen rendered in the platform in a running Kogito process.